<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1968</h1><p class="ref-lede">1968 in world history: 24 events, 9 births, 11 deaths, 2 works, 1 state founded and 4 that came to an end. 241 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1964">1964</a><a href="/years/1965">1965</a><a href="/years/1966">1966</a><a href="/years/1967">1967</a><a href="/years/1968" aria-current="page">1968</a><a href="/years/1969">1969</a><a href="/years/1970">1970</a><a href="/years/1971">1971</a><a href="/years/1972">1972</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warsaw.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-5">5 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Alexander Dubček takes charge in Czechoslovakia</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Dubček replaced Antonín Novotný as leader of the Czechoslovak Communist Party. His reforms, 'socialism with a human face', inspired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a>, ended by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> invasion in August.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-13">13 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Johnny Cash performs at Folsom State Prison</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Johnny Cash played two shows for inmates at Folsom Prison in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, recorded for a live album. 'At Folsom Prison' revived his career and became one of the best-known albums in country music.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-23">23 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">North Korea seizes the USS Pueblo</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">North Korean vessels captured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">US Navy</a> intelligence ship off Wonsan, killing one sailor. The 82 surviving crew were held for eleven months, and the ship is still held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-30">30 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Tet Offensive begins in South Vietnam</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">North Vietnamese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-cong">Viet Cong</a> forces attacked towns and bases across South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> during the lunar new year truce. Though repelled, the offensive undermined American confidence in the war.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-16">16 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">First 911 emergency call made in Alabama</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">In Haleyville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, Senator Rankin Fite made the first call on the new 911 emergency number. A single, easily remembered number spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> over the following decades.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-16">16 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">US soldiers massacre villagers at My Lai</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">American troops killed some 350 to 500 unarmed civilians, mostly women, children and old men, in a South Vietnamese hamlet. Exposed in 1969, the massacre deepened opposition to the war.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-31">31 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Lyndon B. Johnson says he will not seek re-election</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">In a televised address on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, Johnson announced a partial bombing halt and said he would not run again. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-nixon">Richard Nixon</a> won the presidency that November.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-3">3 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Martin Luther King gives his 'Mountaintop' speech</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">In Memphis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a> told supporters he had 'seen the Promised Land' but might not get there with them. It was his last speech; he was assassinated the following evening.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-4">4 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Martin Luther King Jr. is assassinated in Memphis</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a> was shot dead on the balcony of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> Motel, where he had come to support striking sanitation workers. Riots broke out in more than a hundred American cities.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-5">5 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Robert F. Kennedy is shot in Los Angeles</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Minutes after winning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> primary, Senator Kennedy was shot by Sirhan Sirhan at the Ambassador Hotel. He died the next day, two months after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> King's assassination.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/london" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-8">8 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">James Earl Ray is arrested at Heathrow</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> King's killer was caught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Heathrow travelling on a false Canadian passport. He pleaded guilty in 1969 and was sentenced to 99 years.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/moscow" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moscow.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-20">20 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Warsaw Pact armies invade Czechoslovakia</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Hundreds of thousands of Soviet-led troops crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> overnight to end <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-dubcek">Alexander Dubček</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a> reforms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> soon claimed the right to intervene in any socialist state.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mexico-city.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-2">2 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Mexican troops open fire on students at Tlatelolco</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Soldiers and police fired on a student rally in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plaza-de-las-tres-culturas">Plaza de las Tres Culturas</a>, ten days before the Olympic Games opened. Estimates of the dead range from about 30 to several hundred; the government long concealed the toll.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-21">21 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Apollo 8 launches for the Moon</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Frank Borman, Jim Lovell and Bill Anders lifted off on the first crewed flight to leave Earth's orbit. On Christmas Eve they orbited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhu">Moon</a> and photographed 'Earthrise'.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-24">24 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Apollo 8 orbits the Moon</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Frank Borman, Jim Lovell and Bill Anders became the first people to orbit the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhu">Moon</a>. They photographed 'Earthrise' and read from Genesis in a Christmas Eve broadcast seen by millions.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prague-spring.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Prague Spring was the period of reform in Czechoslovakia that began when Alexander Dubček became party leader in January 1968.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact-invasion-of-czechoslovakia" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact-invasion-of-czechoslovakia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Crisis</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact-invasion-of-czechoslovakia">Warsaw Pact invasion of Czechoslovakia</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Warsaw Pact invasion of Czechoslovakia began on the night of 20–21 August 1968, when troops of the Soviet Union, Poland, Hungary and Bulgaria entered the country to end Alexander Dubček's Prague Spring reforms.</p></div></li></ol></section>
